payload.explainer_en:
Tamil Nadu levies professional tax under the Tamil Nadu Tax on Professions, Trades, Callings and Employments Act, 1992, but the tax is collected by local bodies, not by a state department. Municipal corporations, municipalities and panchayats each collect it within their own area, so the employer pays the local body where the establishment is located, and the slabs can differ from one local body to another.

The table shown here is the Greater Chennai Corporation schedule, revised with effect from the second half of 2024-25. Tax is charged on half-yearly income. Up to Rs. 21,000 for the half-year there is no tax. Above that the tax for the half-year is Rs. 180 up to Rs. 30,000, Rs. 425 up to Rs. 45,000, Rs. 930 up to Rs. 60,000, Rs. 1,025 up to Rs. 75,000, and Rs. 1,250 above Rs. 75,000.

The year has two halves: April to September and October to March. The tax for April to September is paid by 30 September, and the tax for October to March by 31 March. The highest slab, Rs. 1,250 for each half, gives Rs. 2,500 for the year, which is the constitutional ceiling on professional tax.

Because the slab depends on income for the whole half-year, the deduction is usually made once, in the last month of the half, after the half-year's salary is known. An employee who joins mid-way through a half-year is assessed on the salary actually earned in that half.

What to do in payroll:
- record the local body for each office and site, not just the state;
- use the Greater Chennai slabs only for establishments within the Corporation's limits, and take the schedule of the other local body for other locations;
- total each employee's income for April to September and October to March, and apply the slab to that total;
- deduct and pay by 30 September and 31 March, and keep the local body's receipt.

